From 1aac6b089e16a160831ffebf807dfd7d8b029538 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: PyScotch <pyscotch@noreply.invalid>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 13:15:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 037/240] Bugfix: avoid deadlocks and duplicate output of
 forked (de)compression tasks [report R. Perry]

When threads are not available (COMMON_PTHREAD_FILE undefined), each
(de)compressed stream is handled by a forked child process. A child
inherits the pipe ends that its father holds for the tasks launched
before it: the write end of every earlier compression pipe and the
read end of every earlier decompression pipe. Hence, an earlier
compression task never sees the end of its stream until the later
child exits, while the father waits for the earlier child first in
`fileBlockClose()`: a program with two compressed output files hangs.
Moreover, children left with `exit()`, which flushes the stdio buffers
inherited from the father: any data pending in e.g. stdout at fork
time was written once more by each child.

Make `fileCompress()` and `fileDecompress()` receive the array of file
descriptors and the index of the stream to process, so that the child
closes the pipe ends of all the (de)compression tasks launched before
it, and leave the child with `_exit()`. The thread implementation is
not affected.

Extend test_common_file_compress with an optional second output file,
and run it with two compressed outputs in the check rules.
